  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe current optimization method relies on local search from multiple starting points, which may get stuck in local optima. We address this by combining multi-resolution optimization with a hybrid global-local search strategy. The key idea is to start with a coarse grid (n=188) using a global optimizer (differential evolution) to escape local minima, then refine the solution on a finer grid (n=376) with a local optimizer (SLSQP). This leverages the fact that solutions on coarser grids can serve as good initial points for finer grids, while global search ensures we don't miss the global minimum.\n\nThe bottleneck addressed is the limited exploration of the solution space. By using differential evolution for global exploration and SLSQP for local refinement, we increase the chances of finding the global minimum. We also incorporate interpolation to transfer solutions between resolutions while maintaining feasibility.\n\nMathematical specification\nWe minimize:\n   C_5 = max_k [ \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx ]\nover h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] with \u222b h(x) dx = 1.\n\nDiscretization:\n   Let n_points be the number of samples (188 or 376).\n   dx = 2.0 / n_points.\n   h is represented as a vector of length n_points.\n   The objective is computed as:\n        correlation = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n        C_5 = (max(correlation) * dx)\n\nConstraints:\n   0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n   \u2211h[i] = n_points/2\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Initialization:\n   - If parent solution (n=188) is provided, use it as initial guess\n   - Otherwise, generate random initial points\n\n2. Coarse grid optimization (n=188):\n   - Use differential evolution with:\n        bounds = [(0,1)] * n_points\n        objective = max(np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full')) * dx\n        Penalize constraint violations with large coefficient\n   - Track best solution and its C_5 value\n\n3. Fine grid refinement (n=376):\n   - Interpolate coarse grid solution to 376 points\n   - Adjust integral to satisfy \u2211h = n_points/2\n   - Use SLSQP with:\n        bounds = [(0,1)] * n_points\n        equality constraint: \u2211h = n_points/2\n        objective = max(np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full')) * dx\n\n4. Comparison:\n   - Keep best solution from both resolutions\n   - Return solution with lowest C_5\n\nSearch choices:\n- Coarse grid: 10000 evaluations with differential evolution\n- Fine grid: 10000 evaluations with SLSQP\n- Penalty factor: 1e6 for constraint violations\n- Initial population for DE: 100 individuals\n\nBudget and robustness:\n- Total evaluations: ~20000\n- Dominant cost: correlation computations (O(n^2))\n- Time estimates: ~100s for coarse grid, ~100s for fine grid\n- Fallback: Return best solution found if time expires\n\nValidation:\n- Check 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- Verify \u2211h = n_points/2\n- Re-evaluate objective function\n- Handle numerical instability by clipping values\n\nReturn contract:\n- h_values: 1D array of floats\n- c5_bound: float (minimum C_5 found)\n- n_points: int (number of samples)\n\nThe solution is feasible if:\n   np.all(0 <= h_values <= 1) and abs(sum(h_values) - n_points/2) < 1e-5\n\nThe objective is computed as:\n   correlation = np.correlate(h_values, 1-h_values, mode='full')\n   c5 = (np.max(correlation) * dx)\n\nThe interpolation method preserves the integral by scaling:\n   h_fine = np.interp(new_x, old_x, h_coarse)\n   h_fine = h_fine * (n_coarse/2 / (sum(h_fine)*dx)) * dx\n\nThis approach balances global exploration with local refinement, leveraging multi-resolution optimization to improve the solution quality.",
